end  with his kids at  night in their 800 square foot home but  even though he was able to work from  home this past year  the portillos still dreamt of owning a  big house the kind they still can't  afford in the bay area  but now with their ten thousand dollar  grant and the blessing of josue's  employer  the fortios which includes josue's  parents say they'll be heading to the  ozark mountains in a few months  where an average home costs less than  270 000  70 cheaper than san jose when we went to  go visit arkansas it felt like a  different country  it just felt like a secret place that we  found  you know like a gym the portillos are  among the growing numbers of remote tech  workers fleeing the broader bay area  and especially san francisco in fact  nearly 40 percent more people moved out  of san francisco during the pandemic  months  compared to the same period a year  earlier the largest increase of any  major city in the country  according to data from moving company  updater  most moved within california but these  states were also top destinations  while cities like austin denver and  seattle are enticing bay area tech  employees more than ever before  this comes as some of the biggest tech  companies like twitter facebook and  salesforce  san francisco's largest private employer  have all announced more permanent remote  work options  i just never had given a thought to  arkansas but if smaller less known  places like northwest arkansas want to  compete for these remote workers  they have to sweeten the deal with cash  and tax incentives in the tens of  thousands  this is going to be amazing like tulsa  oklahoma did  though its ten thousand dollar grants  launched in 2018 the program saw a  dramatic  rise in applicants since the pandemic it  now has more than 500 participants  living and working in tulsa  a newer program called choose topeka  pays up to 15  000 and has brought in 30 workers in  just over a year  right out of the gate it was it exploded  